## How it works

`path_start` and `path_end` are the boundary markers that make "where do paths
begin?" and "where do users leave?" answerable as ordinary event questions — a
[Step Matrix](/docs/widgets/step-matrix) anchored at `path_end`, or a
`cart → path_end` edge on the [Transition Graph](/docs/widgets/transition-graph)
that reads as "abandoned here".

Before:

- u1: `home → cart`
- u2: `home`

```python
stream.add_start_end_events()
```

After — each marker copies the timestamp of the event it brackets, so a
single-event path gets both at the same moment:

| user_id | event | timestamp |
|---|---|---|
| u1 | `path_start` | 10:00 |
| u1 | home | 10:00 |
| u1 | cart | 10:01 |
| u1 | `path_end` | 10:01 |
| u2 | `path_start` | 10:00 |
| u2 | home | 10:00 |
| u2 | `path_end` | 10:00 |

## Deprecated

Don't call this before analysis. `transition_graph`, `step_matrix`,
`step_sankey` and the boundary-aware metrics and patterns add the markers on
the fly, each for the `path_col` it is given. Calling it upfront freezes one
definition of a path into the data — per-user markers would sit in the middle
of most sessions after [`split_sessions`](/docs/data-processors/split-sessions)
— so those tools raise `PathBoundariesPresentError` on an eventstream that
already contains `path_start`/`path_end`. Drop the call and pass `path_col` to
the tool instead:

```python
stream.transition_graph(path_col="session_id")
```

